  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 248 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nSurface high pressure has slid to our east allowing winds to become \nsoutheasterly. With an added southerly component to the wind field, \nwarm air advection has commenced. A gradual warming trend will \ncontinue through tomorrow. High temperatures may climb into the 80s \non Wednesday.\n\nA broad trough skirts to our north late Wednesday into Thursday with \nan associated surface low over the Hudson Bay. Though displaced well \nto our north, this low will drag a surface cold front through the \nOhio River Valley. Cold air advection takes hold on Thursday with a \ncool-down anticipated. Highs dip back into the low 70s both Thursday \nand Friday; lows once again into the 40s. Patchy fog is possible \nduring the overnights with efficient radiational cooling.\n\nThe pattern repeats with surface high pressure arriving and then \npassing to our northeast on Friday. Winds regain a southeasterly \ncomponent allowing gradual warming to begin again. Highs climbing \ninto the upper 70s to near 80 are possible this weekend. Clear skies \naside from occasional cirrus are expected through Saturday.\n\nDry conditions combined with daytime relative humidity values near \n30 percent may allow for increased fire weather potential through \nSaturday. Surface wind speeds are likely to remain light, thus \nmitigating the threat.\n\nSUNDAY ONWARD\n\nWarming will continue into Sunday depending on whether a disturbance \nover the Gulf lifts north or northeastward. Guidance is beginning to \nhone in on the idea of tropical development over the southwestern \nGulf and taking the resulting system northward in conjunction with a \ncut off upper-level low over Texas. This is a highly uncertain \nsituation, since multiple factors are in play that have yet to \ndevelop.\n\nFirst, the tropical cyclone may not develop at all and simply remain \na tropical wave / moisture plume. Second, the upper-level low over \nTexas fails to materialize or is too fast/slow. Any combination of \nthese may dramatically affect the outcome for Indiana. Recent model \ntrends have been towards the idea of tropical development with an \neventual merger between the upper low and tropical cyclone/remnants.\n\nWe can boil it down into two scenarios, however. One where it rains \nand one where it does not. Should moisture lift northward into \nIndiana, whether associated with a weak or strong low, then \ntemperatures are likely to be suppressed and rainfall chances high. \nShould the system fail to develop or miss entirely, then above-\naverage warmth continues into the new week. These systems are within \na larger-scale ridge, so avoiding any precipitation will lead to a \ncontinued warming trend.",
